Validity of Mathematical Formulations

Most of the mathematical expressions in the paper are based on well-known physics, but there are issues in their application and consistency:

    Bekenstein–Hawking Entropy (Eq. 1): The paper correctly cites the black hole entropy formula $S_{BH} = \frac{k_B c^3 A}{4 G \hbar}$​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    , which indeed shows entropy is proportional to the horizon area $A$. This formula is a cornerstone of black hole thermodynamics and is well-supported by theory​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . However, when plugging in numbers, the paper lists $S_{BH} \approx 7.01 \times 10^{54}$ J/K for a “black hole” without specifying the mass​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    . This value of $S_{BH}$ is inconsistent with the 10-solar-mass black hole example later in the text: a 10 $M_\odot$ black hole actually has an entropy on the order of $10^{56}$ J/K, about 20 times higher​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . The given $S_{BH}$ ($7\times10^{54}$ J/K) would correspond to a black hole of roughly 2–3 $M_\odot$, not 10 $M_\odot$. Yet the paper compares this to a Schwarzschild volume for a 10 $M_\odot$ black hole ($1.08\times10^{14}$ m³)​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    . This mismatch suggests an arithmetic or reference error – the entropy and volume used are not for the same black hole mass. Consequently, the numeric surface entropy density $\rho_S^{surface} \approx 6.40\times10^{44}$ J/K/m²​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    is underestimated by the same factor (it should be $\sim10^{46}$ J/K/m² if using a 10 $M_\odot$ black hole’s entropy). While these discrepancies do not invalidate the conceptual argument (the orders of magnitude are so extreme that the conclusion stands qualitatively), it indicates lack of internal consistency in the example calculations.

    Neutron Star Entropy: The paper uses $S_{NS} \approx 3.61 \times 10^{34}$ J/K​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    for a neutron star. This order of magnitude is plausible for a neutron star’s entropy, which is indeed far lower than a black hole’s. (For context, the Sun’s entropy is of order $10^{35}$ J/K​
    en.wikipedia.org
    , and a cold neutron star—being a more ordered, compact object—would have an entropy somewhat lower than the Sun’s despite similar mass.) The huge ratio $S_{BH}/S_{NS} \sim 1.94\times10^{20}$ is credible: it reflects the well-known fact that a black hole has vastly more entropy than any “normal” star or compact object of comparable mass​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . This ratio is essentially illustrating the Bekenstein bound — black holes are believed to be maximum entropy objects for their size/energy​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . The use of this ratio in defining a “collapse factor” $K_{collapse}$ is reasonable, but calling it an “empirical analysis” is a stretch, since these values come from theoretical formulas rather than direct experiment. No derivation is given for why this particular ratio leads to $k_c = 0.001005$; it appears to be a parameter chosen to fit the scenario (perhaps by back-computing what fraction of $T_{\rm Planck}$ yields the observed entropy jump – but the paper does not show that calculation explicitly).

    Critical Temperature and Entropy Density: The introduction of the Chajar Constant $k_c \approx 0.001005$ (dimensionless) and the corresponding $T_c = k_c,T_{\rm Planck}$​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    is a central speculative element. The validity of this concept is questionable because it’s not derived from first principles – it’s essentially posited. The paper then uses $T_c$ to compute a thermal entropy density $\rho_S^{volume} = \frac{4}{3}a,T_c^3$​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    . Here $a$ is the radiation constant (presumably $a=\pi^2 k_B^4/(15\hbar^3c^3)$ or $7.56\times10^{-16}$ J/m³·K⁴ for photon gas). The formula $\rho_S = \frac{4}{3}aT^3$ is indeed the entropy density of a photon gas at temperature $T$ (the factor $4/3$ is correct for blackbody radiation in thermal equilibrium)​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    . Using this formula at $T_c$, the paper finds a minimum volume $V_{required} = S_{BH}/\rho_S^{volume}$ needed to hold the black hole’s entropy if it were distributed in a hot radiation-filled region​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    . The result, $V_{required} \approx 1.53\times10^{-19}$ m³ for $S_{BH}=7.01\times10^{54}$ J/K​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    , is astronomically small – about $10^{33}$ times smaller than the actual black hole’s volume of $\sim10^{14}$ m³​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    . Mathematically, the steps here are straightforward and the disparity is real: even if one allowed an extremely high temperature (0.1% of Planck temperature, or ~$10^{29}$ K), a stellar-mass black hole’s entropy cannot be accommodated in any normal three-dimensional system without exceeding known entropy densities. In fact, black hole entropy per volume is enormously higher than that of an ordinary system – consistent with the idea that black holes saturate the Bekenstein entropy bound​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . The arithmetic in this section is generally sound (aside from the aforementioned choice of $S_{BH}$). One caveat: at temperatures as high as $10^{29}$ K, all kinds of particle degrees of freedom (quark-gluon plasma, electroweak particles, etc.) would be active, potentially increasing the entropy density beyond the photon-gas estimate. The paper’s use of the photon gas formula is a simplification – it gives a lower bound on entropy density. Including more degrees of freedom would make $V_{required}$ even smaller, strengthening the paper’s point. So the conclusion (extreme implausibility of volumetric storage) is qualitatively valid. However, it should be noted that no actual physical system can reach $10^{29}$ K and remain a “normal” plasma without gravitational collapse – so the calculation is largely hypothetical, bordering on tautological (i.e. it assumes conditions under which a black hole would form, then “discovers” the volume must be tiny).

    Surface Entropy Density: The paper correctly computes the horizon area $A = 4\pi r_s^2$ and the surface entropy density as $S_{BH}/A$​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    . This simply recovers the fact that $S_{BH}$ scales with $A$, so the “entropy per unit area” is on the order of $k_B/4\ell_P^2$ (using the Bekenstein–Hawking formula). Plugging in their numbers gives $\rho_S^{surface} \approx 6.4\times10^{44}$ J/K/m²​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    , which (as noted) is underestimated due to the entropy mismatch, but if one uses the correct entropy for a 10 $M_\odot$ BH, it would be $\sim1.3\times10^{46}$ J/K/m². In either case, the value is enormous. The paper states this is “consistent with the holographic principle and quantum gravity theories”​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    , which is true in the sense that many quantum gravity approaches accept that information/entropy of a black hole is encoded on the surface (an idea supported by numerous studies in string theory and quantum gravity​
    en.wikipedia.org
    ). Numerically, there’s no contradiction here; it’s an output of known formulas. The paper doesn’t go further to derive any new equations – it stops at highlighting the contrast between $\rho_S^{volume}$ and $\rho_S^{surface}$ in support of the premise.

In summary, the mathematical steps themselves are mostly valid and grounded in standard thermodynamics (blackbody entropy) and black hole physics (area law). The glaring issue is the inconsistent use of parameters for the black hole example, which appears to be an oversight. This doesn’t negate the qualitative result but is a scientific inaccuracy that should be corrected for consistency. Additionally, the introduction of the Chajar Constant lacks a derivation – it’s effectively an assumed parameter rather than a proven result. The paper would have benefitted from explicitly showing how 0.001005 was arrived at (e.g. solving $V_{required}=V_{BH}}$ or some criterion), but no such derivation is given, making it somewhat ad hoc.
Consistency with Established Physical Laws and Principles

The ideas presented are broadly inspired by established physics – notably the second law of thermodynamics, Bekenstein’s entropy bound, and the holographic principle – but we must examine if any claims conflict with known laws:

    Second Law of Thermodynamics: Nothing in the paper explicitly violates the second law; in fact, the motivation is to uphold the second law in the context of gravitational collapse. It is well-known that if one were to simply “hide” matter (and its entropy) inside a black hole, the second law would seem violated unless the black hole is assigned entropy​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . Bekenstein and Hawking resolved this by showing black holes do carry entropy proportional to their area, ensuring the total entropy (outside + black hole) never decreases​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . The paper’s scenario is consistent with this: as a star collapses to a black hole, the entropy increases massively, satisfying $ΔS>0$ for the isolated system. The author explicitly calls the transition irreversible​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    , which aligns with the second law (irreversible = entropy production). There is no suggestion that entropy ever decreases; instead, entropy is conserved or enhanced by redistributing it to a different “store” (the horizon). In fact, the entropy “collapse” is essentially a mechanism to preserve the second law – it ensures that the enormous entropy of a black hole is accounted for on the surface when volumetric methods fail. Therefore, there’s no violation of the second law here. The term “entropy collapse” might sound like entropy vanishing, but the content makes it clear that isn’t the case – entropy is still present (even amplified), just constrained in where it can reside.

    Bekenstein Bound and Holographic Principle: The arguments are highly consistent with the Bekenstein bound, which states that the maximum entropy $S_{\max}$ in a region of space (of given boundary area or given volume and energy) is proportional to the area of the boundary (on the order of $A/4\ell_P^2$ in units of $k_B$)​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . In fact, for a black hole, the Bekenstein bound is saturated (the bound becomes an equality)​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . The paper essentially rediscovers this idea by showing any attempt to pack $S_{BH}$ into a normal volume leads to an impossibility – implying the black hole is the ultimate entropy-packed state for that region. This matches the widely accepted notion that a black hole represents a state of maximum entropy for a given volume​
    preposterousuniverse.com
    . As Sean Carroll succinctly put it, “a black hole is a state of maximum entropy for a given volume”​
    preposterousuniverse.com
    . The fact that the black hole’s entropy scales with area (not volume) is one of the key pieces of evidence for the holographic principle in theoretical physics​
    en.wikipedia.org
    . The paper explicitly notes that black hole entropy scaling with surface area is often seen as evidence of a holographic universe​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    . By proposing a concrete “mechanism” (the entropy projection at a critical temperature), the author is working within the spirit of established principles, not against them. In short, the area-scaling of entropy and the need for a surface description are consistent with known laws and principles. There is no contradiction here; if anything, the paper reinforces these principles with a specific hypothesis.

    Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics: Aside from the second law, one might wonder about the applicability of equilibrium thermodynamics at such extremes. The paper treats entropy in a very thermodynamic way (e.g. using a temperature and entropy density). In reality, a collapsing star to a black hole is a highly dynamic, general-relativistic process, not a quasistatic thermal process. The concept of a well-defined temperature $T_c \sim 1.4\times10^{29}$ K inside the collapsing matter is speculative – no known physical process in collapse leads to thermal equilibrium at anywhere near this temperature. In known astrophysics, core-collapse supernovae and neutron star formations reach temperatures of order $10^{11}$–$10^{12}$ K, and maybe briefly higher in some scenarios, but nowhere close to $10^{29}$ K. A black hole’s formation, according to general relativity, leads to a singularity (classically infinite density) – classical theory breaks down before any “temperature” like 0.001 $T_{\rm Planck}$ is achieved in a conventional sense. So, while no fundamental law is broken, the paper’s assumption that one can assign a meaningful $T_c$ to trigger entropy projection is at odds with our usual understanding. It’s a theoretical extrapolation that borders on speculation, since quantum gravity effects (not standard thermodynamics) would dominate near such extremes. The second law and thermodynamic relations hold in a broad sense, but the path to equilibrium might not exist in a literal way here.

    General Relativity and Quantum Mechanics Reconciliation: The paper claims its “entropy-first view” helps reconcile GR and QM at the edge of collapse​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    . This is a strong claim but very high-level. There’s no explicit demonstration of reconciliation beyond stating that if entropy is handled via quantum surface degrees of freedom, one might avoid inconsistencies. This touches on the information paradox (a core GR vs QM issue), though the paper doesn’t mention it explicitly. The idea of black hole interiors being “entangled, stable, and 2D at fundamental scales”​
    file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
    is an allusion to quantum descriptions of black holes (for example, holographic dualities or ideas like “ER = EPR” entanglement). These notions are theoretical but not established laws. There is no proven law that black hole interiors must become two-dimensional; it’s a hypothesis motivated by the success of holographic duality in certain models. Thus, while the paper is not violating any known law by suggesting this, it is venturing into conjecture. It remains consistent with the letter of known physics (since known physics simply doesn’t describe the Planckian interior regime), but it’s not something derivable from known physics either.

    Hawking Radiation and Energy Considerations: One thing notably absent is any discussion of Hawking radiation or energy conservation. The paper’s focus is entropy placement rather than energy, so this might be fine. However, a critical reader might wonder: if entropy is projected to the horizon at $T_c$, what is the implication for the black hole’s Hawking temperature (which for a stellar BH is actually extremely low, $\sim 10^{-7}$ K)? The paper doesn’t address this apparent discrepancy between an interior/plasma temperature vs. Hawking (surface) temperature. In standard physics, black holes radiate as if they had a temperature $T_H \propto 1/M$ (which for large $M$ is tiny). The introduction of a high critical temperature might be seen as conflicting with the idea that a large black hole is cold. However, one can reconcile this by noting $T_c$ is not an actual temperature the black hole has after formation; it’s more like the temperature at which a hypothetical dense matter configuration could no longer hold entropy and must collapse. After collapse, the system is a black hole with a cold Hawking temperature externally. So there’s no direct contradiction, but the paper doesn’t clarify this. It treats $T_c$ as a sort of phase-transition point, not as a sustained temperature of the black hole. This is a subtle point where clarification is needed: the scenario should be viewed as a thought experiment (if you try to keep compressing and heating matter, at ~$10^{-3}T_{\rm Planck}$ something new happens) rather than a literal statement about the black hole’s physical temperature. This nuance is important to remain consistent with known black hole thermodynamics.

In conclusion, the paper’s claims do not overtly violate any known physical laws like the second law or energy conservation, but they do push beyond the regime where classical thermodynamics is applicable. The core idea – that an upper bound on volumetric entropy storage leads to an area-law behavior – is in line with established theory (Bekenstein bound and holography)​
en.wikipedia.org
​
preposterousuniverse.com
. The notion of a specific temperature threshold for this transition is not part of standard theory, but since standard theory doesn’t provide a detailed mechanism for black hole entropy, this proposal exists in a grey area of speculation. It complements known principles without disproving them, though it assumes a new detail (the value $k_c$) not found in conventional physics.
