Distinguishing Speculation from Established Science

The paper presents a mix of well-established physics and new speculative hypotheses. It is important to clearly separate which parts are firmly supported and which are conjectural:

    Established Science in the Paper: The following points in the paper rest on a strong foundation of accepted physics:

        Black hole entropy scales with area. This is a confirmed result from theoretical physics​
        en.wikipedia.org
        and is not in dispute. The paper uses this as a starting point.

        Black holes have enormous entropy compared to ordinary objects. This is also well-established. For example, a 1 $M_\odot$ black hole has $S\sim10^{54}$ J/K while a star of 1 $M_\odot$ is around $10^{35}$ J/K​
        en.wikipedia.org
        . The paper’s highlighting of the entropy gap (20 orders of magnitude) is an accepted fact, not a new claim.

        Holographic principle interpretation: The idea that the universe (or black hole interiors) might be “holographic” – i.e. that information in a volume can be represented on a boundary surface – is a mainstream theoretical idea in quantum gravity​
        en.wikipedia.org
        . The paper’s assertion that its results support a holographic view is in line with what many physicists suspect (though this principle itself comes from theoretical arguments rather than experimental proof).

        Irreversibility and thermodynamics: The general thermodynamic reasoning (that an irreversible change occurs and entropy must be accounted for to obey the second law) is standard. The paper’s reasoning that the entropy must go somewhere (and thus likely onto the horizon) to avoid paradox is grounded in the logic that led to black hole thermodynamics in the first place​
        en.wikipedia.org
        .

    These elements are not speculative; they are either observationally supported (no violations of thermodynamics observed) or theoretically well-motivated. The paper largely treats these correctly.

    Speculative or Novel Ideas: Several aspects of the paper are new proposals or interpretations by the author, and these go beyond the established consensus:

        The Chajar Constant ($k_c \approx 0.001005$): This is an entirely new constant defined by the author​
        file-1kwcbevmrazyyhvluhvhxy
